Tab Count Chrome Extension
==========================

This repository contains the source code for the **Tab Count** Chrome extension.
The extension displays the number of open tabs, lets you manage them, and uses
Manifest V3 with a service worker background script.

Chrome Web Store: <https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/tab-count/cfokcacdaonnckdmopmcgeanhkebeaio>

Attribution
-----------

This fork is seeded entirely from !sh's Tab Count project:
<https://github.com/developerish/tab-count>

That repository is the source of the extension, including its Manifest V3 port.
Duplicate-tab detection and stale-tab closing were contributed there by rgluga.

The further changes that keep those features working after Chrome stops the
service worker were made with help from Auto, a Cursor agent.

Manifest V3
-----------

The background context is a service worker. Chrome stops that worker when it is
idle, so features that live only in memory, or that are registered after an
async startup, do not keep running. This extension is written so those features
still work after the worker stops:

- Tab, window, alarm, and notification listeners are registered when the
  service worker starts, before any `await`. Duplicate detection reads the
  saved setting each time a tab URL changes.
- Last-used times are stored in `chrome.storage.session`. Auto-close is a
  `chrome.alarms` job every 60 minutes. It closes tabs that have been idle
  longer than the chosen number of days.
- Settings are stored in `chrome.storage.local`. The popup and options page
  copy any leftover extension `localStorage` values across once.
- A duplicate tab asks with a Chrome notification, with the choices
  "Switch to existing" and "Keep both". A service worker cannot call
  `confirm()`.
